        "val": {
          "first": {
            "seq": 9,
            "text": "Research how to programmatically control Windows Virtual Desktops from Rust using the `windows` or `windows-sys` crates, targeting Windows 11 build 26200.\n\nI need concrete, factual answers to these questions. Use web searches and, if useful, inspect the local windows-sys crate source at:\nC:\\Users\\hoang\\.cargo\\registry\\src\\index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f\\windows-sys-0.61.2\n\nQUESTIONS:\n\n1. What is the documented / publicly known COM interface for Virtual Desktop control on Windows 11? Specifically:\n   - `IVirtualDesktopManager` (documented, in `CLSID_VirtualDesktopManager`) — what does it actually support? I believe it can test whether a window is on the current desktop and move a window to a desktop by GUID, but CANNOT enumerate or switch desktops. Confirm or correct this.\n   - The undocumented `IVirtualDesktopManagerInternal` interface — what methods does it expose (e.g. SwitchDesktop, GetDesktops, CreateDesktop, MoveWindowToDesktop)? What is the CLSID/IID, and is it stable on Windows 11 26200 (25H2)?\n   - Is there a newer documented API on recent Windows 11 (e.g. `IVirtualDesktopManagerInternal2`, or something in the WinRT/Windows.UI.Shell or Windows.Win32 namespaces)?\n\n2. For the UNDOCUMENTED interface specifically: vtable layout matters. Report the exact method ORDER in the vtable, because the interface changed between Windows 10 builds and Windows 11 builds and calling the wrong slot crashes. If the vtable differs between Windows 10 and Windows 11, describe how implementations typically detect which layout to use (e.g. checking OS build number, probing).\n\n3. Are these interfaces available in the `windows` or `windows-sys` crates as pre-generated bindings, or must they be hand-declared? If hand-declared, give the exact Rust `#[repr(C)]` vtable and IID/CLSID GUID values for Windows 11.\n\n4. What are the known practical limitations and failure modes? Consider: whether switching desktops programmatically animates, whether it requires the calling process to be in the foreground, whether it works from a background process, whether it breaks when Explorer restarts, and whether windows moved between desktops fire WinEvents (EVENT_OBJECT_CLOAKED / UNCLOAKED) that a tiling compositor watching those events would observe.\n\n5. Is there a RECOMMENDED alternative approach that avoids the undocumented API entirely? For example: simulating the keyboard shortcut (Ctrl+Win+Left/Right) via SendInput to switch desktops, and using the documented `IVirtualDesktopManager::MoveWindowToDesktop` for moving windows. What is the tradeoff of that approach — specifically, can you still enumerate desktops or know which one is active?\n\nReport your findings as a concise technical brief with:\n- A clear recommendation for the best approach\n- Exact API signatures / GUIDs where you found them (cite URLs)\n- The specific risks, with your confidence level on each claim\n- Explicitly label anything you could NOT verify as unverified rather than guessing\n\nDo not write any code files. Just report findings. Be rigorous about distinguishing documented facts from community reverse-engineering.\nYour parent agent id is \"session-36e052ed-7748-45e0-8292-3d759597ba0f\". Before you finish, send your result to that agent with send_message({ agent_id: \"session-36e052ed-7748-45e0-8292-3d759597ba0f\", message: \"<self-contained result>\" }). The parent shares your workspace but does not automatically receive your transcript, tool output, or reasoning. Send earlier messages as well when a finding changes what the parent should do next; sending a message does not end your turn."
          },
